NAME
       useradd - create a new user account

SYNOPSIS
       useradd [OPTIONS] LOGIN
       useradd -D

DESCRIPTION
       useradd creates the account LOGIN: a line in /etc/passwd (name, UID,
       primary group, comment, home directory, shell), a line in /etc/shadow
       (the password, locked until one is set) and, on RHEL 9 by default, a
       private group with the same name and number in /etc/group, and the
       home directory /home/LOGIN filled with copies of the files in
       /etc/skel. The account cannot log in until it has a password: set one
       with passwd LOGIN.

       Values not given on the command line come from /etc/login.defs (UID
       range, password aging defaults, whether to create the home directory
       and the private group) and /etc/default/useradd (home base, shell,
       skeleton directory). useradd -D prints those defaults.

       New ordinary users get the first free UID from 1000 up (UID_MIN in
       login.defs); system accounts (-r) get one below 1000.

OPTIONS
       -u, --uid UID
              Use this UID instead of the next free one.

       -g, --gid GROUP
              The primary group (name or GID). It must exist. Without -g a
              private group named after the user is created.

       -G, --groups GROUP1[,GROUP2...]
              Supplementary groups (comma separated, no spaces). They must
              exist.

       -c, --comment TEXT
              The comment field (GECOS), usually the person's full name.

       -d, --home-dir DIR
              The home directory, instead of /home/LOGIN.

       -m, --create-home
              Create the home directory (the RHEL 9 default).

       -M, --no-create-home
              Do not create the home directory.

       -s, --shell SHELL
              The login shell. /sbin/nologin makes an account that cannot log
              in interactively (for services, or a user who only uses files
              over the network).

       -e, --expiredate YYYY-MM-DD
              The account stops working on this date.

       -f, --inactive DAYS
              Days after a password expires before the account is locked.

       -r, --system
              Create a system account (UID below 1000, no home directory, no
              aging).

       -k, --skel DIR
              Copy the starting files from DIR instead of /etc/skel.

       -N, --no-user-group
              Do not create a private group; use the default group instead.

       -p, --password HASH
              Set an already encrypted password. Plain text does not work
              here: use passwd or chpasswd instead.

       -o, --non-unique
              With -u, allow a UID that another account already has.

       -D, --defaults
              Print (or with other options, change) the defaults.

EXAMPLES
       Create the user harry, with the password redhat:

           useradd harry
           echo redhat | passwd --stdin harry

       Create sarah with UID 2001, in the supplementary group admins, with a
       full name:

           groupadd admins
           useradd -u 2001 -G admins -c 'Sarah Lee' sarah

       Create an account that cannot log in to a shell:

           useradd -s /sbin/nologin backupuser

       Check the result:

           id sarah
           getent passwd sarah

FILES
       /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group, /etc/gshadow
              The account databases.

       /etc/login.defs, /etc/default/useradd
              Defaults for new accounts.

       /etc/skel/
              Files copied into each new home directory.

SEE ALSO
       usermod(8), userdel(8), passwd(1), chage(1), groupadd(8), id(1),
       login.defs(5), passwd(5), shadow(5)
